    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-258",
      "problem": "The function $g(x)$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14189990/assets/f7051a6b235fdaec0b4bf7f53d5cda95d48b07e6c59ceb03a345f09ff9402749.svg)\nIf $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=2,$ what is the value $b?$",
      "worked_solution": "From the definition of $g(x)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\sqrt{bx+3})=\\sqrt{2b+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x) & =g(2) \\\\ \\sqrt{2b+3} & =5 \\\\ 2b+3 & =25 \\\\ 2b & =22 \\\\ b & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
